<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marie Louise, Duchess of Parma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Marie Louise of Austria · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">House of Habsburg-Lorraine</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marie Louise was an Austrian archduchess, daughter of Emperor Francis I, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1810 and bore his son, Napoleon II. After Napoleon's fall the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave her the Duchy of Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla for life. She ruled it until her death in 1847, after which Parma passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marie-louise-duchess-of-parma.jpg" alt="Marie Louise, Duchess of Parma" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Baptiste Paulin Guérin / After François Gérard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empress_Marie_Louise_of_the_French.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marie Louise was born at the Hofburg Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 12 December 1791, the eldest child of Archduke Francis, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> the next year. She was brought up to detest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Revolution, influenced by her grandmother Maria Carolina, whose sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> had died in it. The imperial family had to leave Vienna in 1805 and again in 1809. She learned English, French, Italian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Spanish, and her parents allowed her almost no contact with men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> in October 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> wanted an heir and a bride from a leading dynasty. His wish to marry a sister of Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and at the urging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Prince Metternich</a> a match with Marie Louise was proposed. Asked for her consent, she answered that she wished only what her duty commanded. They married by proxy on 11 March 1810 and in a civil ceremony on 1 April, and she became Empress of the French. Reserved in public, she never interfered in politics. On 20 March 1811 she bore a son, the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, later Napoleon II.</p><p>She served as regent when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> was away, though he took all decisions. She left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 29 March 1814, and after his abdication returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> with her son. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fontainebleau-1807">Treaty of Fontainebleau</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave her the duchies of Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla for her lifetime only, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> did not want a descendant of Napoleon to hold them by right.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Marie Louise entered Parma on 18 April 1816 with Count Adam Albert von Neipperg, an Austrian officer she had met in 1814, who became her chamberlain and lover and ran daily government on instructions from Metternich. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> died on 5 May 1821 she married Neipperg, who died in 1829. In 1831 an uprising inspired by the Carbonari forced her out of the city for a time, and Austrian troops crushed it. She then married Charles-René de Bombelles, who reformed the duchy's finances, on 17 February 1834. Her son died of tuberculosis in 1832. She died of pleurisy on 17 December 1847 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>; Parma then passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marie Louise, Duchess of Parma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century <b>Marie Louise</b> sponsored public works, including the Teatro Regio, opened in 1829, and a bridge over the Taro.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna gave Lucca to Maria Luisa of Spain, former regent of the Kingdom of Etruria, as compensation for losing Parma, which went to Napoleon's widow <b>Marie Louise</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lucca">Duchy of Lucca</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Parma, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1827" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marie Louise, Duchess of Parma"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1827">What was here in 1827? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
